<script>
'use strict';
const STAGES=[
  {title:'The Expedition',skill:'Recognition & noticing',deck:'Meet the language of contribution, cooperation, and self-reliance before the trail breaks.',
   story:`<p>In the spring of 1823, Hugh Glass joined a fur-trading expedition backed by William Henry Ashley. The Missouri River was the highway, but the country around it was neither empty nor without history. The expedition moved through the homelands of Indigenous nations whose communities, trade networks, and political decisions shaped what could happen next.</p><p>In June, Arikara warriors attacked Ashley’s party near present-day Mobridge. Glass survived. A letter he wrote afterward to the family of John S. Gardner, who was killed, still survives. The expedition regrouped and continued under Andrew Henry. For now, survival was a collective undertaking: every person brought something to the table.</p>`,